{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אך כאשר יאכל הצבי והאיל", :translit "akh ka'asher ye'achel ha'tzvi ve'ha'ayal", :gloss "only as will be eaten the gazelle and the deer"} {:he "אך מיעוטא הוא", :translit "akh mi'uta hu", :gloss "only is a restriction it"} {:he "שאינו חייב במתנות", :translit "she'eino chayav be'matanot", :gloss "that he is not obligated in gifts"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Rabbinic hermeneutic: the word אך ('only') signals a restriction (mi'ut) limiting the scope of the law."}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 12:21 permits slaughtering and eating kosher animals that are not sacrificial 'as the gazelle and the deer are eaten.' The preceding verse (Deuteronomy 12:15) had already stated such meat may be eaten 'in all your gates' and established the rule that the non-sacred animal is exempt from priestly gifts — specifically, the zeroa (shoulder), lechayayim (jaws), and keivah (stomach) mandated in Deuteronomy 18:3 for animals slaughtered 'within your gates.' The word אך at the start of Deuteronomy 12:22 is read by the Ba'al HaTurim as a mi'ut (restrictive particle) reinforcing that exemption, since the entire context (Deuteronomy 12:15–22) addresses non-sacral slaughter ('בכל אות נפשך תזבח ואכלת בשר').", :sources ["Deuteronomy 12:15" "Deuteronomy 12:21" "Deuteronomy 12:22" "Deuteronomy 18:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Rabbinic hermeneutic: the word אך ('only') signals a restriction (mi'ut) limiting the scope of the law.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:19.821381770Z", :model "glm-5.2"}